// 98 FAQs grouped into 14 categories.
const categories = {
  general: 'General DPDPA',
  applicability: 'Applicability',
  consent: 'Consent',
  rights: 'Data Principal Rights',
  obligations: 'Data Fiduciary Obligations',
  sdf: 'Significant Data Fiduciary',
  crossborder: 'Cross-Border Transfer',
  penalties: 'Penalties & Enforcement',
  breach: 'Breach Notification',
  governance: 'DPO & Governance',
  vendor: 'Vendor Management',
  sector: 'Sector-Specific',
  timelines: 'Implementation & Timelines',
  engagement: 'Consulting Engagement'
};

const faqs = [
  // General DPDPA (7)
  { cat: 'general', q: 'What is the DPDP Act 2023?', a: 'The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDPA) is India\'s comprehensive data protection law, governing how organisations collect, process, store and share the digital personal data of individuals in India. It establishes the roles of Data Fiduciary, Data Processor and Data Principal, sets out consent and notice requirements, grants Data Principals specific rights over their own data, creates the Data Protection Board as an enforcement body, and sets financial penalties for non-compliance reaching up to ₹250 crore for the most serious violations. It replaces the more limited data protection provisions that previously existed under India\'s IT Rules, and represents the country\'s first dedicated, comprehensive privacy statute. Many of its operative details — breach notification timelines, Consent Manager registration criteria, and similar procedural matters — are being filled in through the draft DPDP Rules, 2025, which are expected to be finalised and phased in over time. Practically, this means the Act sets the principles and obligations, while the Rules set the specific mechanics organisations must follow to comply with them.' },
  { cat: 'general', q: 'When did the DPDP Act come into force?', a: 'The DPDP Act received Presidential assent in August 2023, but most of its substantive provisions are being brought into force in phases, tied to the finalisation of the DPDP Rules that operationalise them. Certain provisions — largely relating to the constitution of the Data Protection Board — have commenced ahead of others. Organisations should not treat the Act as a future concern simply because full commencement is phased: the underlying obligations reflect data protection expectations that are prudent to meet regardless of the exact date a specific provision becomes formally enforceable, and the compliance work itself takes months, not days, to complete properly.' },
  { cat: 'general', q: 'How is the DPDPA different from GDPR?', a: 'The DPDPA shares family resemblance with the EU\'s GDPR — both centre on consent, notice, and individual rights — but differs in important ways. The DPDPA does not create a separate "special category" of sensitive personal data with distinct rules, as GDPR does. Its cross-border transfer regime is permissive by default, restricted only where the government specifically notifies a restriction, versus GDPR\'s adequacy-first, restrictive-by-default model. The DPDPA introduces the novel Consent Manager intermediary, which GDPR does not have an equivalent to. Penalty structures, exemption scope, and enforcement mechanics also diverge meaningfully. Organisations that have already built GDPR compliance have a head start conceptually, but a line-by-line mapping exercise is essential — assuming GDPR compliance automatically satisfies the DPDPA is a common and risky mistake.' },
  { cat: 'general', q: 'Does the DPDPA apply to data processed outside India?', a: 'Yes, in a specific circumstance: the Act applies to the processing of digital personal data outside India if that processing is in connection with any activity related to offering goods or services to Data Principals within India. This extraterritorial reach means an overseas company selling to Indian consumers, even without a physical presence in India, can fall within the Act\'s scope. Organisations operating global platforms with an Indian user base should assess this exposure carefully rather than assuming the Act is only a concern for India-incorporated entities.' },
  { cat: 'general', q: 'Is there a threshold below which small businesses are exempt from DPDPA?', a: 'The Act allows the Central Government to notify exemptions for specific classes of Data Fiduciaries, including on grounds such as the volume and nature of personal data processed — which could, in principle, cover certain categories of startups or small businesses. However, no broad, automatic small-business exemption exists in the Act\'s core text itself, and any such exemption would need to be specifically notified. Organisations should not assume exemption based on size alone; the obligations apply based on the nature and volume of personal data actually processed, not just headcount or revenue.' },
  { cat: 'general', q: 'What is the difference between the DPDPA and the draft DPDP Rules?', a: 'The Act is the primary legislation passed by Parliament, setting out the framework of obligations, rights, and enforcement mechanisms. The draft DPDP Rules, 2025 are subordinate legislation issued by the government that fill in the Act\'s operative detail — specific breach notification timelines, Consent Manager registration criteria, DPIA requirements, and procedural mechanics for exercising rights. Rules can be updated more readily than the Act itself as practical experience accumulates, so organisations need to track both layers, and treat the Rules as the primary source for exact procedural requirements rather than relying on the Act\'s more general language alone.' },
  { cat: 'general', q: 'Who enforces the DPDP Act?', a: 'The Data Protection Board of India is the primary enforcement body — receiving breach notifications, investigating complaints, issuing directions, and imposing penalties through its adjudicatory function. The Board operates largely as a digital-first office. Sector regulators (RBI, IRDAI, TRAI, healthcare regulatory bodies) continue to enforce their own existing data-related rules independently and in parallel — DPDPA enforcement does not replace or override sectoral regulatory oversight, meaning organisations in regulated sectors answer to both the Board and their sector regulator.' },

  // Applicability (7)
  { cat: 'applicability', q: 'Does the DPDPA apply to my company if we only process employee data?', a: 'Yes. Employee personal data — names, contact details, payroll, performance records, background checks — is personal data like any other, and processing it makes your organisation a Data Fiduciary with respect to that data. The Act does recognise employment-related processing as a Legitimate Use in many cases, meaning explicit consent is not always the operative requirement, but other obligations — notice, security safeguards, accuracy, and rights fulfilment — still apply to employee data. Many organisations focus compliance efforts exclusively on customer-facing data and materially under-govern their HR systems as a result.' },
  { cat: 'applicability', q: 'Are B2B companies with no consumer product covered by the DPDPA?', a: 'Yes, in a more limited but real way. Even a purely B2B company processes personal data — employee records, the personal contact details of client-side individuals (names, emails, phone numbers of counterpart employees), and candidate data during hiring. The scope of exposure is typically smaller than a consumer-facing business, but "we\'re B2B, this doesn\'t apply to us" is a common and incorrect assumption we routinely correct during Gap Assessments.' },
  { cat: 'applicability', q: 'Does DPDPA apply to data collected before the Act was passed?', a: 'The Act\'s obligations generally attach to the processing of personal data on or after the relevant provisions come into force, regardless of when the data was originally collected — meaning legacy data holdings are not simply grandfathered out of scope. In practice, this means most organisations need to assess their historical data holdings — marketing lists, old CRM records, archived files — against current obligations, which is exactly the work our Legacy Data Remediation service is built to do.' },
  { cat: 'applicability', q: 'Does the DPDPA apply to anonymised or aggregated data?', a: 'No — genuinely anonymised data, from which no individual can reasonably be identified even in combination with other available information, falls outside the Act\'s scope, since it is by definition no longer "personal data." The operative test is whether re-identification is reasonably likely given the specific dataset and the organisation\'s access to other identifying information, not simply whether an obvious identifier like a name has been removed. We test anonymisation claims rather than accepting them at face value, since poorly executed anonymisation frequently remains re-identifiable.' },
  { cat: 'applicability', q: 'Are non-profits and educational institutions covered?', a: 'Yes — the Act applies based on the nature of processing activity, not the organisation\'s legal or tax status. A non-profit or educational institution that processes donor, beneficiary, student, or staff personal data is a Data Fiduciary with respect to that data, subject to the same core obligations as a commercial entity, unless a specific exemption applies to the particular activity in question.' },
  { cat: 'applicability', q: 'Does the DPDPA cover data about deceased individuals?', a: 'The Act\'s core protections are generally oriented toward living Data Principals, but it does introduce the novel Right of Nomination, allowing a Data Principal to designate someone to exercise their rights specifically in the event of death or incapacity — which implicitly acknowledges post-death handling of an individual\'s data as a live operational question organisations need a process for, even if the deceased individual\'s own rights framework differs from a living Data Principal\'s.' },
  { cat: 'applicability', q: 'What about data of foreign nationals visiting or working in India?', a: 'The Act\'s scope is generally framed around processing of digital personal data within India, and processing outside India connected to offering goods or services to individuals within India — the nationality of the Data Principal is not itself the determining factor. A foreign national\'s personal data processed by an Indian entity, or processed in connection with services offered to them while in India, falls within scope on the same basis as any other Data Principal\'s data would.' },

  // Consent (7)
  { cat: 'consent', q: 'What makes consent valid under the DPDPA?', a: 'Valid consent under the DPDPA must be free, specific, informed, unconditional and unambiguous, given through clear affirmative action, and limited to the personal data necessary for the specified purpose. It must be obtained after clear notice describing what data is collected and why. A single, bundled "I agree to Terms & Privacy Policy" checkbox covering multiple unrelated purposes typically fails the specificity and unconditional requirements. Consent must also be as easy to withdraw as it was to give — a standard many legacy consent flows do not meet.' },
  { cat: 'consent', q: 'Can consent be implied through continued use of a product?', a: 'Generally, no — the DPDPA requires clear affirmative action to constitute valid consent, which implied or passive continued use does not satisfy. Certain scenarios are instead handled through the Act\'s Legitimate Uses provisions (such as data voluntarily provided for a specified purpose where the individual has not objected), but these are narrower and more conditional than a general "if they kept using it, they consented" assumption, and should not be relied on as a substitute for proper consent capture.' },
  { cat: 'consent', q: 'Do we need consent for every single data field we collect?', a: 'You need a valid basis — consent or an applicable Legitimate Use — for every purpose you process personal data for, and notice covering the specific data and purpose involved. In practice this is organised at the purpose level rather than field-by-field: a single consent action can validly cover multiple data fields collected for one specific, disclosed purpose, but that same consent cannot be stretched to cover an unrelated second purpose without separate, specific disclosure and consent for it.' },
  { cat: 'consent', q: 'Can we pre-tick a consent checkbox?', a: 'No — pre-ticked checkboxes do not constitute clear affirmative action, which the DPDPA requires for valid consent. The Data Principal must take a deliberate, active step to indicate agreement; a default-on toggle they would need to actively switch off does not meet this standard, regardless of how the surrounding notice language is worded.' },
  { cat: 'consent', q: 'How specific does a consent purpose need to be?', a: 'Specific enough that a Data Principal can meaningfully understand and separately agree to it — "process your loan application" and "share your contact details with our marketing partners" are two genuinely distinct purposes and should be two distinct consent decisions, not folded into a single "provide you services" catch-all. The right level of granularity is a judgment call we make jointly with clients during purpose-taxonomy design, balancing genuine specificity against overwhelming users with dozens of micro-consents.' },
  { cat: 'consent', q: 'What is a Consent Manager and do we need to integrate with one?', a: 'A Consent Manager is a Data Protection Board-registered intermediary that lets a Data Principal manage consent across multiple Data Fiduciaries from one interface. Integrating with a registered Consent Manager is not currently a blanket requirement for most Data Fiduciaries, but designing your own consent architecture — structured Consent Artefacts, purpose-level granularity — to be interoperable with that model is sensible forward planning as the ecosystem develops and may become expected practice, if not a formal requirement, over time.' },
  { cat: 'consent', q: 'What happens if a Data Principal withdraws consent?', a: 'Once consent is withdrawn, you must cease processing that Data Principal\'s data for the withdrawn purpose within a reasonable time, and ensure any Data Processor you engaged for that purpose does the same. The withdrawal itself must be at least as easy to execute as giving consent was in the first place. Designing the technical propagation path — so a withdrawal recorded in one system actually halts processing everywhere that data subsequently flowed to — is often the harder half of this obligation, and a common gap we find in fragmented data architectures.' },

  // Data Principal Rights (7)
  { cat: 'rights', q: 'What rights does a Data Principal have under the DPDPA?', a: 'A Data Principal has the right to access a summary of their personal data and the processing activities undertaken, the right to correction and erasure of their data, the right to grievance redressal, the right of nomination (designating someone to act on their behalf in the event of death or incapacity), and the right to withdraw consent at any time. Alongside these rights, the Act also specifies certain duties for Data Principals, such as not making frivolous or false complaints.' },
  { cat: 'rights', q: 'How do we verify the identity of someone making a rights request?', a: 'The Act does not prescribe a single fixed verification method, leaving organisations to design a proportionate process — strong enough to prevent fraudulent requests (someone impersonating another person to access or erase their data), but not so onerous it becomes a practical barrier to genuine requests. We calibrate this to each client\'s risk profile as part of our Data Principal Rights Fulfillment Setup service, since a bank and a content app reasonably warrant different verification rigor.' },
  { cat: 'rights', q: 'Can a Data Principal request we delete all their data immediately?', a: 'A Data Principal can request erasure of personal data that is no longer necessary for the purpose it was collected for, but a Data Fiduciary may retain data where required by law — for example, tax or financial record-keeping requirements — even after an erasure request. The correct response is rarely a simple binary "delete everything" or "refuse everything"; it typically requires assessing each retained data category against whether a specific legal retention obligation genuinely applies to it.' },
  { cat: 'rights', q: 'What is the Right of Nomination and how common are requests likely to be?', a: 'The Right of Nomination lets a Data Principal designate another individual to exercise their rights in the event of the Data Principal\'s death or incapacity — a genuinely novel right not commonly found in comparable global frameworks. Request volume is likely to be low in absolute terms for most organisations initially, but the absence of any defined process for it is a conspicuous gap in a Gap Assessment precisely because so few organisations have built one, making it an easy, high-visibility fix.' },
  { cat: 'rights', q: 'Do we need a self-service rights portal, or can we handle requests manually?', a: 'A dedicated self-service portal is not a strict legal requirement — a well-designed intake form with a defined internal routing and SLA process can satisfy the underlying obligation at moderate request volumes. As volume grows, the operational cost of manual handling typically makes a portal worthwhile on efficiency grounds alone, but that is a business decision layered on top of, not required by, the base compliance obligation.' },
  { cat: 'rights', q: 'What happens if we miss the deadline to respond to a rights request?', a: 'A Data Principal whose request goes unanswered, or unsatisfactorily answered, within the applicable timeframe can escalate the matter as a grievance, and ultimately to the Data Protection Board. Beyond the specific request, a pattern of missed deadlines is exactly the kind of operational evidence that can turn an isolated complaint into a broader Board inquiry into your organisation\'s overall compliance posture — which is why SLA design and monitoring, not just having a process on paper, matters.' },
  { cat: 'rights', q: 'Do minors have the same rights as adult Data Principals?', a: 'A child\'s rights under the Act are exercised by their parent or lawful guardian rather than the child directly, reflecting the broader framework of Verifiable Parental Consent that governs processing of a child\'s data generally. Organisations serving children need a defined process for a parent or guardian to exercise rights on the child\'s behalf, which is distinct from — and typically requires stronger identity verification than — a standard adult rights-request process.' },

  // Data Fiduciary Obligations (7)
  { cat: 'obligations', q: 'What are the core obligations of a Data Fiduciary?', a: 'A Data Fiduciary must give clear, itemised notice before or at the time of seeking consent, obtain valid consent (or rely on an applicable Legitimate Use), implement reasonable security safeguards proportionate to risk, ensure the accuracy and completeness of data used for decisions or shared onward, retain data no longer than necessary for its purpose, notify the Data Protection Board and affected Data Principals of any personal data breach, provide an accessible grievance redressal mechanism, and honour Data Principal rights requests within a reasonable timeframe. Together these obligations form the backbone of nearly every service we deliver.' },
  { cat: 'obligations', q: 'Do we need a written data protection policy?', a: 'The Act does not mandate a single named "data protection policy" document by that specific title, but demonstrating adherence to obligations like reasonable security safeguards, purpose limitation, and retention discipline in practice is far easier — and far more defensible in an inquiry — with documented internal policies governing them, rather than relying on informal or undocumented practice that varies by team.' },
  { cat: 'obligations', q: 'What counts as "reasonable security safeguards"?', a: 'The Act does not prescribe a fixed technical checklist; "reasonable" is calibrated to the sensitivity of the data involved and the risk of harm from a breach. In practice this typically includes access controls, encryption of sensitive data at rest and in transit, logging and monitoring, incident response readiness, and vendor security due diligence — proportionate to what a specific system actually holds and processes, rather than a flat, one-size-fits-all control set applied uniformly regardless of risk.' },
  { cat: 'obligations', q: 'Do we need to appoint a DPO even if we\'re not a Significant Data Fiduciary?', a: 'A full Data Protection Officer appointment is a mandatory requirement specifically for Significant Data Fiduciaries. Every Data Fiduciary, regardless of size, must still designate a contact person able to address Data Principal questions about their data. Many mid-market organisations voluntarily appoint a fractional DPO — such as through our DPO-as-a-Service offering — well before SDF designation, because the governance discipline it brings has value independent of the strict legal minimum.' },
  { cat: 'obligations', q: 'How long can we retain personal data?', a: 'Only as long as necessary to fulfil the purpose for which it was collected, or as required by another applicable law, whichever is longer — not an indefinite default. Once the purpose is fulfilled and no other legal retention basis applies, the data should be erased. This requires a defined retention schedule per data category rather than a single organisation-wide rule, since different data types are tied to different purposes and different legal retention requirements.' },
  { cat: 'obligations', q: 'Are we obligated to conduct an annual privacy audit?', a: 'A periodic data audit is a mandatory obligation specifically for Significant Data Fiduciaries, conducted by an independent data auditor. Other organisations are not statutorily required to run an annual audit, but doing so voluntarily — and re-assessing your DPDP Readiness Level periodically — is strong practice for catching drift as products, teams and vendor relationships change over time.' },
  { cat: 'obligations', q: 'What obligations apply specifically when we share data with a third party?', a: 'When sharing personal data with a Data Processor, you remain accountable for that processing and must ensure the Processor operates under proper instructions and adequate safeguards through a Data Processing Agreement. When sharing with another Data Fiduciary — a business partner making independent decisions about the data — you generally need a valid basis (consent or Legitimate Use) covering that specific sharing purpose, and must disclose the sharing as part of fulfilling a subsequent right to access request.' },

  // Significant Data Fiduciary (7)
  { cat: 'sdf', q: 'How does an organisation know if it will be designated a Significant Data Fiduciary?', a: 'The Central Government notifies specific Data Fiduciaries, or classes of Data Fiduciaries, as Significant based on factors including the volume and sensitivity of personal data processed, risk of harm to Data Principals, potential impact on India\'s sovereignty and integrity, risk to electoral democracy, security of the state, and public order. There is no fully self-assessed threshold published as a bright-line number; organisations operating at meaningful scale in data-intensive sectors should proactively assess their exposure against published criteria and precedent rather than waiting for formal notification.' },
  { cat: 'sdf', q: 'What additional obligations apply to a Significant Data Fiduciary?', a: 'SDFs must appoint a Data Protection Officer based in India who reports to the board or an equivalent governing body and serves as the point of contact for grievance redressal, appoint an independent data auditor, conduct periodic Data Protection Impact Assessments and data audits, and undertake other measures the Central Government may specify — including evaluation of the algorithmic systems used in processing. This is a materially higher governance bar than the general obligation set applicable to all Data Fiduciaries.' },
  { cat: 'sdf', q: 'Can a Significant Data Fiduciary use a fractional DPO?', a: 'The Act specifically requires an India-based DPO reporting to the board for SDFs — a role that implies a level of continuous, senior engagement generally associated with a dedicated appointment rather than a light-touch fractional arrangement. Organisations expecting or approaching SDF designation should plan a transition path from any fractional arrangement to a dedicated appointment; we help design and time that transition rather than treating it as an overnight requirement.' },
  { cat: 'sdf', q: 'What is the algorithmic accountability requirement for SDFs?', a: 'SDFs are required to undertake measures, including evaluating the algorithmic software they deploy for processing personal data, to ensure it does not pose a risk to Data Principants\' rights. In practice this means reviewing recommendation engines, credit-scoring models, fraud-detection systems and similar algorithmic tools for accuracy, bias, and proportionality — an emerging area of scrutiny that intersects with, but is distinct from, general AI governance conversations happening elsewhere in the organisation.' },
  { cat: 'sdf', q: 'Does SDF designation apply to the whole company or a specific product?', a: 'Designation can be made in respect of a Data Fiduciary, or a class of Data Fiduciaries — the precise scope of a given designation depends on how it is notified. Organisations with multiple, meaningfully distinct business units or products should not assume a designation automatically covers or excludes any specific product without checking the actual terms of the notification that applies to them.' },
  { cat: 'sdf', q: 'How often must an SDF conduct a DPIA?', a: 'The Act requires SDFs to undertake DPIAs periodically, with the specific cadence and procedural detail expected to be clarified further through the DPDP Rules. In the absence of a single fixed universal interval published to date, we recommend organisations build an annual DPIA cadence for their highest-risk processing activities as a defensible baseline, adjusting cadence upward for any activity that changes materially between cycles.' },
  { cat: 'sdf', q: 'What happens if an SDF fails to meet its enhanced obligations?', a: 'Non-compliance with SDF-specific obligations carries its own penalty category under the Act\'s schedule, adjudicated by the Data Protection Board through the same inquiry and penalty process applicable to other violations. Given the scale typically associated with SDF designation, the practical exposure — both financial and reputational — from non-compliance at this tier tends to be significantly higher than for a standard Data Fiduciary.' },

  // Cross-Border Transfer (7)
  { cat: 'crossborder', q: 'Can we store customer data on servers outside India?', a: 'Generally yes — the DPDPA permits cross-border transfer of personal data by default, restricted only to countries or territories the Central Government has specifically notified as restricted. This is a more permissive default than many organisations expect, especially those familiar with GDPR\'s adequacy-first model. However, sector-specific localisation rules — most notably RBI\'s requirements for payment system data — apply independently and can require India-only storage regardless of the DPDPA\'s general permissiveness.' },
  { cat: 'crossborder', q: 'How do we know if a country is restricted for data transfer?', a: 'Restrictions are established through specific Central Government notification — there is currently no single, permanent published blacklist that stays fixed indefinitely; the list can be updated. We monitor notified restrictions as a standing part of client engagements and flag exposure if a country your architecture relies on is newly restricted, since transitioning an established data flow can require significant lead time.' },
  { cat: 'crossborder', q: 'Does using an international cloud provider count as a cross-border transfer?', a: 'It can, depending on the specific data centre region the provider processes and stores your data in. Using a global cloud provider\'s India-region infrastructure generally does not trigger cross-border transfer concerns for that specific data; using a non-India region for storage, backup, or processing does. We recommend explicitly confirming with your provider exactly which regions your specific workloads and backups actually reside in — not assuming India-region hosting from a general enterprise agreement alone.' },
  { cat: 'crossborder', q: 'Do we need a data transfer agreement for cross-border transfers?', a: 'The Act itself does not mandate a specific transfer-agreement mechanism analogous to GDPR\'s Standard Contractual Clauses. However, having contractual protections in place with any offshore processor — covering security safeguards, breach notification, and use restrictions — remains sound risk management even where the transfer itself is not currently restricted, and may align with expectations that develop further under the Rules.' },
  { cat: 'crossborder', q: 'Does outsourcing customer support to a BPO in another country count as a transfer?', a: 'Yes — if personal data is accessed or processed by staff located outside India, that constitutes a cross-border transfer for DPDPA purposes, even if no data is permanently stored abroad. Screen-sharing, remote access, and cloud-based support tools accessed from outside India all warrant the same scrutiny as a formal data export would.' },
  { cat: 'crossborder', q: 'How does the DPDPA interact with RBI\'s payment data localisation rules?', a: 'The two regimes apply independently and simultaneously. RBI\'s requirement that payment system data be stored exclusively in India is a sector-specific localisation mandate that predates and operates alongside the DPDPA\'s more general, permissive cross-border framework. A payments company must satisfy both — DPDPA compliance does not substitute for RBI compliance, and vice versa.' },
  { cat: 'crossborder', q: 'What should we do if a country we currently transfer data to gets newly restricted?', a: 'You would need to restructure or unwind that specific data flow within whatever transition period, if any, accompanies the restriction notification. This typically means re-architecting storage or processing to an unrestricted region, which can involve significant engineering lead time — proactive monitoring of notification trends, rather than reactive scrambling after a notification lands, is the better posture, and one we build into ongoing advisory retainers.' },

  // Penalties & Enforcement (7)
  { cat: 'penalties', q: 'What is the maximum penalty under the DPDPA?', a: 'The Act\'s penalty schedule caps the most serious category — failure to take reasonable security safeguards resulting in a personal data breach — at up to ₹250 crore. Other categories of non-compliance, including failure to notify a breach, violations related to children\'s data, and Significant Data Fiduciary-specific failures, carry their own specified caps, generally lower but still substantial, reaching up to ₹200 crore or ₹150 crore for certain categories.' },
  { cat: 'penalties', q: 'Who decides how much penalty to impose?', a: 'The Data Protection Board, through its adjudicatory function, conducts an inquiry into an alleged violation and determines the penalty within the statutory cap for that category of violation, considering factors such as the nature and gravity of the non-compliance, the type of personal data affected, repetitive nature of the breach, and mitigating steps taken by the organisation.' },
  { cat: 'penalties', q: 'Can an organisation appeal a Data Protection Board penalty?', a: 'Yes — a person aggrieved by a Board order or direction can appeal to the Appellate Tribunal, which the Act designates as the Telecom Disputes Settlement and Appellate Tribunal (TDSAT). This provides a defined judicial-style review layer above the Board\'s own adjudicatory decisions.' },
  { cat: 'penalties', q: 'Is there a way to resolve a compliance issue without a formal penalty?', a: 'The Act allows an organisation under inquiry to offer a voluntary undertaking — a commitment to take, or refrain from, specific actions to resolve the matter — which the Board may accept, potentially resolving proceedings on agreed terms rather than through a contested penalty order. This gives organisations a cooperative path to demonstrate good faith remediation.' },
  { cat: 'penalties', q: 'Does the Data Protection Board only act on complaints, or can it investigate proactively?', a: 'The Board can inquire into complaints from Data Principals and can also act on instances of non-compliance that come to its attention through other channels, such as breach notifications it receives directly. Organisations should not assume regulatory exposure is limited only to situations where a specific individual has filed a complaint against them.' },
  { cat: 'penalties', q: 'Are individual employees personally liable for company non-compliance?', a: 'The Act\'s penalty framework is generally directed at the Data Fiduciary or Data Processor entity itself rather than creating a standalone framework of individual criminal liability for ordinary employees. That said, senior officers with clear governance responsibility — particularly a designated DPO or accountable executive — carry meaningful personal accountability exposure in practice, even where the formal penalty falls on the entity.' },
  { cat: 'penalties', q: 'How does penalty exposure factor into deciding whether to invest in compliance now?', a: 'Beyond the direct penalty exposure, non-compliance carries compounding costs: lost deals from failed customer security reviews, stalled funding rounds during diligence, reputational damage from a public complaint or breach, and the far higher cost of remediating under regulatory deadline pressure versus on a planned timeline. Most clients find the business case for proactive compliance investment holds up well before you even factor in the statutory penalty numbers.' },

  // Breach Notification (7)
  { cat: 'breach', q: 'What counts as a notifiable personal data breach?', a: 'A personal data breach is any unauthorised processing, or accidental or unauthorised disclosure, acquisition, sharing, use, alteration, destruction, or loss of access to personal data that compromises its confidentiality, integrity, or availability. This is broader than a "hack" in the popular sense — misconfigured access controls, an accidental email to the wrong recipient, or an internal access failure can all qualify, which is why every organisation needs a clear internal classification framework to triage incidents against this definition quickly.' },
  { cat: 'breach', q: 'How quickly must a breach be reported to the Data Protection Board?', a: 'The draft DPDP Rules propose that the Board be notified without delay upon becoming aware of a breach, with a more detailed follow-up report required within a specified window thereafter. Given how tight the initial window is expected to be, the classification and escalation process needs to exist before an incident occurs — building it during a live incident all but guarantees missing the deadline.' },
  { cat: 'breach', q: 'Do we need to notify affected individuals as well as the regulator?', a: 'Yes — the Act and draft Rules require notifying both the Data Protection Board and affected Data Principals, with the individual notification expected to describe the nature of the breach and its likely consequences in clear, plain language, not technical jargon. Drafting this communication well — accurate and compliant, without triggering unnecessary panic — is a specific skill we build into our breach readiness templates.' },
  { cat: 'breach', q: 'Does every security incident require notification, even minor ones?', a: 'Not necessarily — but every incident needs to be triaged against your breach classification framework quickly enough to make that determination within the notification window, rather than assuming it doesn\'t qualify without a documented assessment. A documented "we assessed this and it did not meet the notification threshold" record is itself valuable evidence of a functioning compliance process.' },
  { cat: 'breach', q: 'What should our breach response plan actually contain?', a: 'At minimum: a classification framework distinguishing notifiable breaches from routine security events, a defined escalation chain naming who is informed in what order, ready-to-adapt notification templates for the Board and for Data Principals, and a tested process — ideally validated through a tabletop exercise — for executing all of this under real time pressure, not just on paper.' },
  { cat: 'breach', q: 'If our vendor causes the breach, who is responsible for notifying?', a: 'The Data Fiduciary generally retains the regulatory notification obligation even when the underlying incident originated with a Data Processor, which is exactly why Data Processing Agreements need explicit clauses requiring the vendor to notify you promptly of any incident on their side — without that clause and a fast vendor-notification obligation, you may not even learn about a breach in time to meet your own deadline.' },
  { cat: 'breach', q: 'Can we test our breach response plan without an actual incident?', a: 'Yes, and we strongly recommend it — a tabletop exercise simulates a realistic breach scenario with your actual team, under time pressure, to test whether the plan works in practice rather than just on paper. It is far cheaper and lower-risk to discover gaps in a simulation than during a real incident, and it is a standard component of our Breach Response & Notification Readiness engagements.' },

  // DPO & Governance (7)
  { cat: 'governance', q: 'Is appointing a DPO mandatory for every organisation?', a: 'A dedicated DPO appointment is mandatory specifically for Significant Data Fiduciaries. Every Data Fiduciary, regardless of designation, must still designate a contact person to handle Data Principal queries. Many organisations voluntarily appoint a DPO — often on a fractional basis — well before any SDF-related requirement applies, because of the governance value it brings independent of the strict legal minimum.' },
  { cat: 'governance', q: 'What does a DPO actually do day to day?', a: 'A DPO typically chairs recurring privacy governance reviews with product and engineering teams, reviews new features and vendor contracts for compliance exposure before launch, owns the grievance redressal and rights-fulfilment process, monitors regulatory developments, and reports on compliance posture to leadership or the board. It is a genuinely operational role, not a purely advisory or ceremonial one.' },
  { cat: 'governance', q: 'Can our General Counsel also serve as DPO?', a: 'There is no formal prohibition, and many organisations do combine the roles, particularly at smaller scale. As data protection obligations grow more operationally intensive — reviewing product launches, running audits, owning incident response — the combined role can become a bottleneck, which is why many organisations eventually separate the functions or bring in fractional dedicated DPO support even without a formal requirement to do so.' },
  { cat: 'governance', q: 'How often should the board receive compliance updates?', a: 'We recommend at minimum an annual formal briefing, with an additional update ahead of any major event that changes your data risk profile — a funding round, IPO process, new market entry, significant product launch, or M&A transaction. Significant Data Fiduciaries, given the board-reporting DPO structure the Act requires, should expect a more frequent cadence than that baseline.' },
  { cat: 'governance', q: 'What governance structure does the Act require for a standard (non-SDF) Data Fiduciary?', a: 'Beyond designating a contact person for Data Principal queries, the Act does not prescribe a specific mandatory governance structure — no required committee, no mandated reporting line — for a standard Data Fiduciary. In practice, however, some form of accountable ownership (even informal, at a smaller organisation) is what makes every other obligation — notice accuracy, consent design, rights fulfilment — actually happen consistently rather than by accident.' },
  { cat: 'governance', q: 'Do we need a privacy committee or can one person own everything?', a: 'At smaller scale, a single accountable owner — whether an internal hire or a fractional DPO — can genuinely run the programme, provided they have real authority to influence product and engineering decisions. As organisational complexity grows, a cross-functional governance forum (legal, product, engineering, security) becomes valuable specifically because privacy decisions increasingly require input none of those functions holds alone.' },
  { cat: 'governance', q: 'How do we keep our compliance programme current as the Rules evolve?', a: 'This requires active monitoring of MeitY notifications and Rules updates, not a one-time compliance project treated as finished. Most of our ongoing engagements — DPO-as-a-Service in particular — include this monitoring as a standing function, specifically because the Rules are still maturing and what counts as compliant today may need adjustment as further detail is notified.' },

  // Vendor Management (7)
  { cat: 'vendor', q: 'Do all our vendors need a Data Processing Agreement?', a: 'Any vendor processing personal data on your instructions should have contractual terms addressing DPDPA-relevant obligations, though the depth of that agreement should scale with the vendor\'s risk tier — a payroll processor handling sensitive employee data warrants a more rigorous agreement than a transactional email delivery service with narrow, low-risk access.' },
  { cat: 'vendor', q: 'What should a DPDPA-aligned Data Processing Agreement include?', a: 'At minimum: instruction-bound processing language confirming the vendor acts only on your documented instructions, breach notification obligations requiring prompt notice to you of any incident, data deletion or return obligations on contract termination, restrictions on further sub-processing without your knowledge or approval, and audit or inspection rights allowing you to verify compliance.' },
  { cat: 'vendor', q: 'Are we liable if our vendor has a data breach?', a: 'The Data Fiduciary generally retains primary regulatory accountability for personal data processed on its behalf, even where the specific incident originates with a vendor. Strong contracts, prompt vendor notification clauses, and regular due diligence reduce your practical exposure significantly, but they do not eliminate the underlying accountability the Act places on the Data Fiduciary.' },
  { cat: 'vendor', q: 'How often should we re-review our vendor relationships?', a: 'We recommend a full due diligence refresh at least annually, supplemented by a lighter-weight review triggered whenever a vendor changes its infrastructure region, subprocessors, or the scope of data it accesses — waiting for the annual cycle to catch a mid-year architecture change is a common and avoidable gap.' },
  { cat: 'vendor', q: 'What about free or low-cost SaaS tools our teams adopt without formal procurement?', a: 'These "shadow IT" tools are a genuinely common and high-risk blind spot — a marketing team adopting a new analytics tool, or a support team trialling a new ticketing platform, without any centralised procurement or compliance review. We specifically screen for this pattern during vendor inventory work, since it\'s exactly the kind of collection point a scan-driven discovery process is designed to surface.' },
  { cat: 'vendor', q: 'Do we need to audit our vendors\' subprocessors too?', a: 'Ideally, yes, at least at a level of visibility — your DPA with a vendor should restrict their ability to bring on new subprocessors without notifying or seeking your approval, and you should periodically confirm what subprocessors are actually in play, since a compliant primary vendor relationship can still carry hidden risk through an under-vetted subprocessor chain.' },
  { cat: 'vendor', q: 'Can we use offshore vendors at all under the DPDPA?', a: 'Yes — the DPDPA\'s cross-border transfer regime is permissive by default, so using offshore vendors is generally permitted unless the specific country is government-restricted or a sectoral localisation rule applies to your data category. The vendor being offshore raises cross-border and due-diligence considerations to manage, but is not itself prohibited.' },

  // Sector-Specific (12, covers BFSI/Healthcare/EdTech/Ecommerce/HR)
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'Does DPDPA compliance satisfy RBI\'s data protection expectations for BFSI entities?', a: 'No — DPDPA compliance and RBI\'s sector-specific regulatory expectations (data localisation for payment systems, IT governance frameworks, cyber-security frameworks for banks and NBFCs) operate independently and must both be satisfied. A BFSI entity needs a compliance programme that maps explicitly to both regimes rather than assuming one substitutes for the other.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'How does DPDPA affect KYC data collection in financial services?', a: 'KYC data collection is generally supportable under Legitimate Uses provisions and specific regulatory mandates rather than requiring standalone consent for the core verification purpose, but any secondary use of that same KYC data — cross-selling other products, marketing — requires separate, specific disclosure and consent, distinct from the KYC collection itself.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'Does DPDPA require special handling for patient health records?', a: 'The DPDPA does not create a distinct statutory "sensitive data" category for health records the way GDPR does, but health data still warrants materially higher operational caution given the potential harm from mishandling, and healthcare providers must separately satisfy medical confidentiality and record-keeping norms under other applicable law alongside DPDPA obligations.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'Can a hospital rely on Legitimate Uses for emergency treatment data processing?', a: 'Yes — the Act specifically recognises processing necessary to respond to a medical emergency, or to provide medical treatment during an epidemic or public health threat, as a Legitimate Use not requiring standard consent. This exemption is scoped to the emergency or public-health context specifically, and should not be extended to routine, non-emergency data processing.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'What is Verifiable Parental Consent and why does it matter so much for EdTech?', a: 'It is the heightened consent standard required before processing a child\'s (under-18) personal data — parental or guardian consent must be genuinely verifiable, not just self-declared. EdTech platforms, by definition serving a substantial population of children, face this requirement pervasively across their user base, along with restrictions on behavioural monitoring and targeted advertising to children, making it one of the most consequential compliance areas for the sector.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'Can EdTech platforms use age-gating alone to satisfy verification requirements?', a: 'Simple self-declared age-gating (a birthdate field a user can freely enter) is a weak verification mechanism unlikely to satisfy a genuinely "verifiable" standard on its own. Stronger patterns typically combine age-gating with an actual parental consent capture step — a verified parental email or a payment-method-linked verification — designed to avoid both under-verification and excessive additional data collection in the process.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'Does bundling marketing consent with checkout count as valid consent for e-commerce?', a: 'No — bundling marketing consent into a single checkbox alongside checkout or Terms acceptance fails the DPDPA\'s specificity and unconditional consent requirements. Marketing consent needs to be a genuinely separate, optional, affirmative choice, distinguishable from the transaction itself, and just as easy to withdraw later.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'Are third-party ad-tech pixels and SDKs a compliance risk for e-commerce sites?', a: 'Yes, often an underestimated one — every ad-tech pixel or analytics SDK embedded in your site or app is effectively a data collection point requiring its own purpose disclosure and, generally, consent before it fires. A scan-driven data discovery exercise frequently surfaces more of these embedded collection points than a site owner is aware exist.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'How should HR teams handle background verification vendor data sharing?', a: 'Background verification typically involves sharing candidate personal data with a third-party verification vendor, which needs a proper Data Processing Agreement addressing instruction-bound processing and data handling standards, alongside clear candidate notice about the verification process itself and what data will be checked and shared.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'Do algorithmic resume-screening tools raise DPDPA concerns for HR-tech?', a: 'Yes — algorithmic screening and scoring of candidates constitutes a form of automated decision-making and profiling that warrants fairness and accuracy review, particularly given the potential for discriminatory outcomes, and is squarely the kind of processing that Significant Data Fiduciaries\' algorithmic accountability obligations are designed to scrutinise, and good practice for any HR-tech provider regardless of designation.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'Do logistics companies need consent from recipients, not just senders, for delivery data?', a: 'Generally, delivery-related processing of a recipient\'s name, address and phone number is supportable as necessary to fulfil a transaction the sender initiated on the recipient\'s behalf, but this should not be stretched to justify unrelated secondary uses of that recipient data, such as adding the recipient to an unrelated marketing list without their own separate consent.' },
  { cat: 'sector', q: 'How should gig-economy logistics platforms handle delivery-partner location tracking?', a: 'Real-time location tracking of delivery personnel processes their personal data and should be disclosed to them clearly as a condition of the platform relationship, with data minimised to what operational need (route optimisation, delivery verification, safety) actually requires, rather than retained indefinitely or repurposed for unrelated analytics without separate disclosure.' },

  // Implementation & Timelines (7)
  { cat: 'timelines', q: 'How long does a typical DPDPA compliance programme take to implement?', a: 'For a mid-market organisation, a full first-cycle programme — Gap Assessment, consent and notice redesign, rights-fulfilment setup, vendor remediation, and initial training — typically runs three to six months from kickoff to substantial completion, depending on organisational complexity, number of products in scope, and how quickly internal stakeholders can engage. Simpler, single-product organisations can move faster; multi-business-unit enterprises typically take longer.' },
  { cat: 'timelines', q: 'What should we do first if we\'re starting from zero?', a: 'Start with a DPDP Gap Assessment. Attempting to fix consent flows, rewrite policies, or redo vendor contracts before you have a clear, evidence-based picture of where your actual gaps are risks solving the wrong problems first, or missing higher-priority issues entirely. The assessment gives you a sequenced, resourced roadmap to work from rather than a scattershot response.' },
  { cat: 'timelines', q: 'Can we phase our compliance work over a longer period if budget is constrained?', a: 'Yes, and a phased roadmap is exactly what our Gap Assessment delivers — findings are scored by both regulatory exposure and remediation effort specifically so lower-cost, high-impact items can be tackled first, with more resource-intensive structural work sequenced into later phases as budget allows.' },
  { cat: 'timelines', q: 'How quickly do the DPDP Rules requirements need to be met once finalised?', a: 'Historically, subordinate rules under Indian legislation are often accompanied by a transition or grace period for compliance, though the specific timeline for the DPDP Rules will depend on the final notification. Organisations that have already built the underlying operational capability — rather than waiting for the Rules to be finalised before starting any work — are far better positioned to absorb whatever specific timeline is ultimately set.' },
  { cat: 'timelines', q: 'Should we wait for the Rules to be finalised before starting compliance work?', a: 'No — the Act\'s core obligations (notice, consent, security safeguards, rights fulfilment) are already substantially clear, and the underlying operational work (building a data inventory, redesigning consent flows, standing up a rights process) takes months regardless of exactly when Rules-level procedural detail is finalised. Waiting simply compresses your timeline once deadlines do land.' },
  { cat: 'timelines', q: 'How long does the DPDP Gap Assessment itself take?', a: 'Typically two to five weeks, depending on the number of products, business units and systems in scope, and stakeholder availability for interviews. A single-product startup can often complete an assessment in two weeks; a multi-business-unit enterprise typically takes closer to five.' },
  { cat: 'timelines', q: 'Once we complete remediation, is the work ever really "done"?', a: 'Not in the sense of a one-time project — new products, new vendors, and evolving Rules all create ongoing compliance surface area. Organisations that reach our Level 3 (Defined) readiness tier and beyond typically shift from project-based engagement to a sustaining retainer model, such as DPO-as-a-Service, specifically to keep the programme current rather than letting it decay after the initial push.' },

  // Consulting Engagement FAQs (7)
  { cat: 'engagement', q: 'How is ReadyDPDP different from a law firm or generalist consultancy?', a: 'A law firm can tell you whether a specific clause or interpretation is legally sound. A generalist consultancy can help with broad process design. ReadyDPDP sits specifically at the intersection of DPDPA legal obligations and operational implementation — we don\'t just advise on what the law requires, we design and help build the actual consent flows, data registers, and fulfilment processes that make compliance real in your systems, informed by direct familiarity with the technical architecture DPDPA compliance actually runs on.' },
  { cat: 'engagement', q: 'Do you replace the need for legal counsel?', a: 'No — we work alongside your legal counsel, not in place of them. Complex legal interpretation questions, contract disputes, and formal regulatory representation remain squarely legal counsel\'s domain. Our work is the operational and technical implementation layer: translating legal requirements into concrete process, architecture, and documentation, and flagging where a genuinely novel legal question needs your counsel\'s direct judgment.' },
  { cat: 'engagement', q: 'What is the relationship between ReadyDPDP and truConsent?', a: 'ReadyDPDP is the consulting and advisory practice powered by truConsent, an IITM-incubated Privacy Intelligence Suite built specifically for DPDP Act 2023 compliance. Where truConsent provides the software platform for organisations that want a technology-driven consent and rights-management solution, ReadyDPDP provides the human advisory layer — assessment, strategy, policy, and hands-on programme design — for organisations that need expert guidance, whether or not they ultimately adopt any specific software platform.' },
  { cat: 'engagement', q: 'Do we need to buy any software to work with ReadyDPDP?', a: 'No — our advisory services are platform-agnostic. We assess your needs and recommend a technical approach appropriate to your scale and existing systems, which may or may not include a dedicated consent management platform. Where a client does want a technology-driven solution, we can advise on the market including truConsent\'s platform, but our recommendations are not contingent on any specific software purchase.' },
  { cat: 'engagement', q: 'How much does a typical engagement cost?', a: 'Pricing depends on organisational complexity, scope, and which specific services you engage — we do not publish a fixed rate card because a single-product startup and a multi-business-unit enterprise have genuinely different needs that a flat number would misrepresent either way. Every engagement starts with a free scoping call, after which we provide a fixed-fee quote before any billable work begins.' },
  { cat: 'engagement', q: 'Can you work with our existing in-house legal and compliance team rather than replacing them?', a: 'Yes — this is in fact our most common engagement model. We typically augment an existing legal or compliance function with the specific DPDPA operational expertise and implementation bandwidth they may not have in-house, rather than displacing their role. Our DPO-as-a-Service offering in particular is designed to integrate directly with an existing internal team.' },
  { cat: 'engagement', q: 'What industries does ReadyDPDP have the deepest experience in?', a: 'Our team\'s deepest sector experience spans BFSI, healthcare, EdTech and e-commerce — sectors where personal data volume, sensitivity, or regulatory overlap create the highest compliance stakes. That said, our core methodology (Gap Assessment, DPDR and CDJR register building, consent architecture design) is sector-agnostic by design and applies directly to any organisation processing personal data, regardless of vertical.' }
];

module.exports = { categories, faqs };
